  {
    "episode_id": 91133,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91133.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 279,
    "turn_text": "Thank you, David. All right. So, yeah, I'm taking your calls 954-889-6410. If you're on Skype, you can Skype in to Nicole Sandler. In the meantime, the rest of the show is up to us. I've got a couple of things, putting aside what's going on with the fighting between, and I hate to say the progressives and the moderates, because you know me, I don't ascribe to the moderate label. I do want to tell you, though, tomorrow I'm going to be speaking with Zach Carter. Zachary decarter who wrote for years for Huffington Post and Huffington Post in case you didn't know was recently bought out by BuzzFeed and they slashed the staff. Now it sucks that some good people like Zach Carter will let go. But Huffington Post, or now Huff Post, sorry, has gotten better editorial wise. So their content is better, but they let go of some good people. Anyway, Zack Carter has a book out dealing with the price of peace and economics and all that. And actually, we taped it earlier today. It's going to run tomorrow. It's really good. We get down and dirty in all of this, you know, biff and infrastructure and, you know, the whole sausage factory that's happening in D.C. right now. So we'll talk about that. But today, what happened is Nancy Pelosi basically said, screw your stinking deal. We're going to vote on this thing on Thursday. Well, let me share with you what Bernie Sanders just tweeted out, if I can find it here. So Bernie wrote, let's be crystal clear, if the bipartisan infrastructure bill is passed on its own on Thursday, this will be in violation of an agreement that was reached within the Democratic caucus in Congress. More importantly, it will end all leverage that we have to pass a major reconciliation bill. That means there will be no serious effort to address the long-neglected crises facing the working families of our country, the children, the elderly, the sick, and the poor. It also means that Congress will continue to ignore the existential threat to our country and planet with regard to climate change. I strongly urge my house colleagues to vote against the bipartisan infrastructure bill until Congress passes a strong reconciliation bill. they need to do it together. The only reason they need to do it together is because of the leverage, leverage. It's what gives, it's the give and take. It's how they work together. And it's the deal they made. And all of a sudden Nancy Pelosi saying, yeah, all right, about that deal. Well, maybe not. Look, what Charlie Chris said, I agree with again, I can't believe I'm saying these words, but I agree. We need both of them. My worry is that if they pass the smaller one first, we're not going to get the other one. And that would suck. I mean, and that's putting it really basic in basic terms. It would be so much worse than sucking. It would be a disaster because it would mean nothing gets better. We need things to get better. So what will it take to get this through? Well, right now, the stumbling block is Kirsten Sinema and Joe Manchin. And I understand that both of them were again summoned to the White House today, so I guess Joe Biden is working them. We all need to work them too. We need to work the phones, especially if you live in West Virginia or Arizona. Now the Arizona Democratic Party said that if Kirsten Sinema fucks this up, they will give a vote of no confidence for her. So she'll be out of a job. But you know what, she's also holding a fundraiser today with five lobbying groups that are against this reconciliation bill. So I don't know what she's trying to do. She told someone in an interview that she wants to emulate John McCain. So what she thinks, that means bucking your party. That means going against the wishes and interests of your constituents, because that's not what John McCain did. John McCain decided to go with the side of sanity. John McCain wasn't willing to fuck up, you know, our access to healthcare to overturn the Affordable Care Act. That's why he did this. Kirsten Sinema, in her little Catholic girl's school outfit, goes on the Senate floor and goes like this to a $15 an hour minimum wage. That's not what John McCain did. She's nothing like John McCain. David, yes.",
