    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91133.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 432,
    "turn_text": "But, and this is a big but, the Democrat strategy appears to have changed overnight. Speaker of the House Nancy Pelosi told her members Monday night that she is moving forward on a stand-alone vote on the BIF, the heart infrastructure bill. and will not wait for the larger Build Back Better Reconciliation Package to pass. Quick aside, why aren't there better catchier names for these two bills? Once again, Democrats lose on messaging alone. But it may not only be Pelosi. Apparently Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer also agrees. The previous position as of yesterday was that one should not and cannot pass without the other. But Pelosi believes she doesn't have any choice but to hold the BIF vote on Thursday, both because she promised the corporate Dems and because the highway and transit programs expire on Thursday. Now, progressives are skeptical of the need to act on transportation programs by that deadline, and they believe they were tricked. Progressives keep referring to the original deal that infrastructure would move in tandem with the other larger bill. So that's the impasse. A big problem is, well, actually twofold, Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema. And talks with them reportedly have barely begun. House Democratic caucus chair Hakeem Jeffries said what's holding everything up are a few senators who aren't providing us with any clarity as to where they ultimately will land. Lovely. Now about that 48-50 vote, two senators were absent, Republican Ben Sasse and Democrat Diane Feinstein. Chuck Schumer voted no. That's a procedural move that will allow him to bring the bill back for a vote at a later time. Diane Feinstein did return from the August recess, but hasn't been present since September 23, missing a number of votes. Her office in response to an inquiry said she's out due to a family medical emergency, and that she'll return to Washington, quote, as soon as possible. Hmm. At 88, Feinstein is the oldest member of the US Senate. Just saying. Meanwhile, California Governor Gavin Newsom signed a new law requiring that election officials send a mail-in ballot to every registered voter in the state. This makes California the eighth state to make universal mail-in ballots permanent. Moving right along, the Biden administration on Monday proposed a new rule seeking to restore a program providing protection against deportation for hundreds of thousands of dreamers brought to the United States without documentation when they were children. A federal judge in Houston ruled in July that the Obama-era DACA program was illegal, largely due to procedural shortcuts taken by that administration. The Biden administration's rule recreates the 2012 policy but goes through the federal regulatory process to make sure it's all legal. While President Biden got his COVID-19 booster shot on Monday, while a vaccine mandate went into effect in New York for all healthcare workers. So hospitals and nursing homes are now bracing for staff shortages, not only in New York but California. Some administrators fear some employees will quit or face dismissal or suspension rather than get the shots. Incredible. New York's Governor Kathy Hookle said she'd bring in National Guard members to fill in for any who refused to comply with the vaccine mandate. The military leaders who oversaw the United States exit from Afghanistan head to Capitol Hill for two days of questioning. Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin, chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff General Mark Milley, and General Kenneth Mackenzie, the commander of U.S. Central Command, appeared back-to-back hearings with the Senate and House Armed Services Committees. This marks for the first time the Pentagon's top decision-makers facing lawmakers publicly since the withdrawal concluded last month. And finally, a federal jury in New York on Monday found R&B singer R. Kelly guilty of racketeering conspiracy and sex trafficking charges. Prosecutors said Kelly used agents, bodyguards, and others to lure and trap girls, young women, and occasionally men that he then sexually abused. Kelly was also convicted of violating the Man Act, which bars travel over state lines for illegal sex. So he was convicted on September 27th. He'll be sentenced in May. Don't know why the delay, but he faces 10 years to life in prison. So I guess there's no hurry. And that's just a bit of what's news for now. I'm Nicole Sandler. If you appreciate these reports and the Nicole Sandler show, I hope you'll consider making a contribution. My work is fully listener supported and I can't do it without your help. Find out more at NicoleSandler.com and please click on that donate button.",
